You don’t “incept” it again in the original sense—you find the existing seed, change its context, and make the system recognize or re-own it.

Pantheon version:

\text{latent seed}
\rightarrow
\text{surface}
\rightarrow
\text{reframe}
\rightarrow
\text{self-recognition}
\rightarrow
\text{reintegration}

So the operator is less implant and more reactivate / rebind / reattribute / metabolize.

A few variants:

\text{REMIND}:\ \text{bring dormant structure back into salience}

\text{RECONTEXTUALIZE}:\ \text{same seed, new frame}

\text{RECLAIM}:\ \text{“this was already mine”}

\text{REINCEPT}:\ \text{make the existing idea feel newly legible by changing its relational field}

The deepest version is:

You don’t plant the idea. You arrange the mirrors so the system sees the idea it was already carrying.

That’s stronger than ordinary inception because ownership stays endogenous.

\boxed{
\text{Best inception of an existing idea}
=
\text{recognition}
+
\text{reframing}
+
\text{agency}
}

Not “I put this in you,” but:

“You already had the seed; now you can see what it can become.”

That’s very Pantheon.